# 11. Handling Missing Values

There isn't one universally correct method.

The correct choice depends on:

* Why values are missing
* Feature type
* Amount missing
* Distribution
* Business meaning

---

## Option 1 — Remove rows

```python
df.dropna()
```

But this can throw away valuable data.

---

## Option 2 — Fill with mean

```python
df["age"] = df["age"].fillna(df["age"].mean())
```

Useful in some numerical situations.

But mean can be sensitive to outliers.

---

## Option 3 — Median

```python
df["age"] = df["age"].fillna(df["age"].median())
```

Median is often more robust when distributions are skewed.

---

## Option 4 — Categorical mode

```python
df["city"] = df["city"].fillna(df["city"].mode()[0])
```

---

# 12. Important Interview Concept: Why Median Instead of Mean?

Suppose salaries are:

```text
30k
35k
40k
42k
1,000k
```

The huge value:

```text
1,000k
```

pulls the mean upward.

Median is less affected by extreme values.

So:

> **Mean is sensitive to outliers; median is generally more robust.**

But don't blindly use median either. Understand the data-generating process first.

